2186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186422152}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "21379b36-910e-5f85-8d70-2437c1f661cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-332918" :question/problem "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+2y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2x(t)-2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above. The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are ${v}_{1}=[1,\\text{ }-2]^{T}$ and ${v}_{2}=[2,\\text{ }1]^{T},$ respectively. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}\\cdot {{field-1}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}\\cdot {{field-2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-3}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\nSince ${e}^{-3t}\\to $ \n{{field-4}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-5}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/61/617f0b123b98086ed902041c1a601d44ac264ab97acfc9e2af25ca330699d98f.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny={{field-6}}\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line.\n\nSince ${e}^{2t}\\to $ \n{{field-7}}\nas $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach \n{{field-8}} \nas $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/6e/6e410f51824c79ee39d8f514b72926ce861108d39df4c1ac58bbb9c2d3210eae.png)\n\nThe {{field-9}} of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/d3/d3449435602d6b160490462ab97545bb2aba0cc7fce498de2df8a226b90e0a49.png)" :question/worked-solution "The general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=-2x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line. \n\n Since ${e}^{-3t}\\to 0$ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach  $\\text{the origin}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/61/617f0b123b98086ed902041c1a601d44ac264ab97acfc9e2af25ca330699d98f.png)\n\nThe line that passes through the origin and is parallel to ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ has the equation \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\nand the straight-line solutions of the form\n${c}_{2}{e}^{2t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$\nlie on that line.\n\n Since ${e}^{2t}\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ these solutions approach  $\\text{infinity}$ as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/6e/6e410f51824c79ee39d8f514b72926ce861108d39df4c1ac58bbb9c2d3210eae.png)\n\nThe $\\text{phase portrait}$ of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/d3/d3449435602d6b160490462ab97545bb2aba0cc7fce498de2df8a226b90e0a49.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186422153 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166916 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ada3a84a-f979-5aee-9422-7acb8e430c11" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Phase Portrait of a System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1efe7af1-942d-5760-9304-56dd958cadaf" :answe